Subject: register VM: in-place string accumulator

What this does:
  Adds regvm_accum_append, a register-path port of the stack VM's
  js_accum_append. When a `s += x` add has its destination register equal to its
  left operand and that register provably holds the only reference to a normal,
  non-atom string, the append happens in place (memcpy + length bump, or a
  realloc with growth slack) instead of allocating a fresh rope.

  The "only reference" test is the DRC state: a register-only value has ref_count
  0 and sits on the zero-count table, while a value any slot owns carries a
  counted reference. Borrowed-register aliases are invisible to the count, so the
  helper additionally scans every location in the frame that can hold a borrowed
  value -- the register file and the argument window -- and refuses on any match.
  Slots own counted references, so a zero count already rules out locals,
  closures, properties and array elements.

  Wired into the four add sites: the threaded T_BODY_RRR macro, the threaded
  regvm_t_binary_cpool handler, the monolith REGVM_BIN_OP macro and the monolith
  L_ROP_binary_cpool. The step helpers (quickjs-regvm-step.inc) are regenerated
  to expose rf->arg_buf/rf->argc to REGVM_BIN_OP.

Why we need it:
  The generic register add duplicates both operands before calling the engine, so
  JS_ConcatString2's in-place test can never fire and every `s += x` allocates a
  new string. That concat memcpy is pdfjs's dominant remaining cost. Measured on
  the deterministic corpus (instructions:u, R4 -> R5): pdfjs -10.1%; every other
  row within +0.5%/-0.8% (noise). pdfjs Octane score 4201 -> 5094.

Needs a bytecode version bump: No

Notes:
  - d and a are register indices (element offsets into fr); the threaded call
    sites shift the raw index<<4 byte offsets the threaded decoder hands out.
  - The right operand is borrowed and is never freed, unlike js_accum_append
    which frees its op2.
  - Under QJS_DRC only; a non-DRC build compiles the helper to `return 0`.

